Conjunctivitis (Viral/Bacterial/Allergic)
Opthalmology & ENT
Conjunctival injection, Ocular discharge, Eyelash matting/crusting

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-014
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, bilateral purulent eye discharge with marked swelling and suspected infectious spread within a household.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported rapid onset of severe, bilateral "red eyes" with copious, thick purulent discharge sealing the eyelids shut, accompanied by significant eyelid swelling. Urgent assessment was required to definitively rule out sight-threatening hyperacute bacterial conjunctivitis (e.g., Neisseria gonorrhoeae), infectious keratitis, or anterior uveitis, and to initiate immediate, aggressive topical antimicrobial therapy to arrest the infection and prevent local transmission prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My eyes are glued shut with this thick, yellow pus, and the whites of my eyes look like raw meat."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 24-hour history of rapidly worsening diffuse bulbar and palpebral conjunctival injection (pink eye). Initially unilateral, it rapidly spread to the other eye. Endorses copious, continuous, thick yellow purulent discharge. Experienced severe matting and crusting of the eyelashes upon waking, requiring warm water to pry the lids open. Reports a persistent gritty, foreign body sensation but denies deep aching pain, photophobia, or sudden vision loss.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently impaired by continuous discharge obscuring vision.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of contagious transmission to roommate.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 | HR: 86 | Temp: 37.3 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, frequently wiping eyes with tissues.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity is normal bilaterally post-blinking to clear discharge. Diffuse, fiery red bulbar and tarsal conjunctival injection present bilaterally. Copious, thick, opaque, yellow-green purulent discharge rapidly reforming at the medial canthus after wiping. Mild to moderate bilateral eyelid edema and superficial erythema. Corneas are brilliantly clear with no focal infiltrates, opacities, or ulcers visible on gross or ophthalmoscopic exam. Pupils equal and briskly reactive. No prominent preauricular lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Tissues piled on coffee table; shared bathroom facilities with roommate.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications. No known allergies.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Bacterial Conjunctivitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Viral Conjunctivitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The sheer volume and opacity of the continuous purulent discharge (viral is typically watery) and the absence of preauricular lymphadenopathy or preceding upper respiratory symptoms.

Anterior Uveitis (Iritis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of severe photophobia, absence of deep ciliary flush (limbal redness), normal pupillary reactivity, and presence of heavy purulent discharge.

Infectious Keratitis (Corneal Ulcer) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear corneas without visible focal white infiltrates and normal visual acuity once discharge is cleared.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Highly contagious)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe, bilateral purulent ocular discharge and profound conjunctival injection. Immediate face-to-face assessment was critical to differentiate a benign surface infection from vision-threatening intraocular or corneal pathologies (uveitis, keratitis). The physical examination confirmed gross bacterial conjunctivitis with an intact, clear cornea. Because of the aggressive nature of the purulent discharge and the high risk of cross-infection, immediate prescription and initiation of broad-spectrum topical antibiotics were mandated. Delaying assessment would have subjected the patient to severe distress, worsening edema, and uncontrolled infectious shedding throughout the household overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Chloramphenicol 0.5% eye drops, 1 drop to both eyes every 2 hours for the first 24 hours, then QID for 5 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: To instantly reduce the bio-burden and promote drop efficacy, the patient must physically wash their hands with soap and water immediately before and after every single drop administration. This forced interruption guarantees hygiene compliance, directly resulting in faster symptom resolution and visibly preventing the roommate from contracting the infection (positive reinforcement through social safety).
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48-72 hours if purulent discharge has not significantly decreased.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops extreme sensitivity to light, a deep aching pain inside the eye itself, blurring of vision that does not clear with blinking, or a white spot on the cornea.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; assumes standard bacterial pathogen.
Clinical Course: The extreme redness and heavy discharge will begin to improve within 24-48 hours of starting the intensive antibiotic drops. The condition is highly contagious until 24 hours of treatment have been completed.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If looking at lights suddenly becomes extremely painful, or your vision stays blurry even after wiping the pus away, you must go to the emergency department immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A strong bacterial infection has taken hold on the surface lining of your eyes. The frequent antibiotic drops will rapidly kill the bacteria, but they must be applied to a clean eye to work properly.
Actionable Steps: Gently wipe away crusts with a clean, disposable tissue before putting the drops in. Do not share towels, pillows, or any facial products with your roommate. Wash hands obsessively.

